  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Depth both ways – three zones",
  "description": "Five against five – four outfield players and a goalkeeper on each team – on a pitch that is clearly longer than it is wide, for example 50 × 30 metres. Split the pitch into three equal zones with cones along the sidelines.\n\nRule: when the team has the ball, there must be at least one outfield player in each zone – someone behind the ball and someone ahead of it. A goal counts double if the last pass went to a player making a forward run in behind. The players take turns to give depth behind, come short and run in behind.\n\nVariation: at most three touches. Play with offside if the team does so in matches. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Give depth at different distances: one close, one far away – both behind and ahead of the ball."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "One comes short, one goes in behind – two players making the same run only give one option."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Start the run in behind when the ball carrier has time to look up, not when they are under pressure."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "On the ball: look for the run in behind first, and play safely backwards if it isn't on."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
